POWER FOR LITIGANTS: WINTER V. NRDC AND PRELIMINARY RELIEF
Winter v. NRDC introduces us to preliminary relief, often the first of many steps along the path to a final judgment in court. A preliminary injunction is a court order that grants some (usually temporary) relief to a party despite the fact that the party’s claims have not been fully resolved; courts issue preliminary injunctions “to protect [a party] from irreparable injury and to preserve the court’s power to render a meaningful decision after a trial on the merits.”* Generally, the goal of a preliminary injunction is maintenance of the status quo pending resolution of the parties’ claims after a full opportunity to litigate. Thus, preliminary relief tends to be negative or prohibitory in character—i.e., forbidding a party from taking an action—rather than mandatory—i.e., compelling action. The standards for granting or denying a preliminary injunction have been developed by courts through cases such as our next case, Winter v. NRDC.

In Winter, environmentalists had sought to prevent the United States Navy from conducting training exercises that could seriously injure whales, dolphins, and sea lions. This clash of environmental and national-security interests led to a court judgment entering a preliminary injunction, and the United States Supreme Court ultimately reviewed this judgment in the decision we are about to read. As you read Winter, try to identify how the Supreme Court describes the requirements for granting a preliminary injunction.

There is much more to this case than just the Supreme Court or standards for preliminary relief, though. The Supreme Court decision came at the end of a complex series of moves and countermoves by the parties to this case, most of which did not occur in the Supreme Court, and some of which did not occur in court at all. Importantly, all the moves and countermoves of the parties to this dispute were procedural maneuvers. As you will see, even the legal basis of the plaintiffs’ lawsuit—their claim that the Navy was violating the law—was about a certain kind of procedure. What was the legal claim? [Hint: it was not that it was against the law for the U.S. Navy to harm marine mammals.] As you read Winter, look out for the many ways in which this case implicates procedure.
